WEBVTT

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.550
Begin with four real observations from
January fourth and fifth, twenty eleven.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:09.970
At two in the morning,
the rental counts were one and two.

00:00:10.280 --> 00:00:11.250
At eight,

00:00:11.400 --> 00:00:14.555
they were one hundred and seventy
nine and one hundred and ninety five.

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:18.140
Calendar inputs can be known in advance,

00:00:18.140 --> 00:00:21.890
while these outcomes become
available after their hours.

00:00:22.460 --> 00:00:26.690
For this small teaching probe,
keep only the hour eight indicator.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:30.710
The later benchmark retains all
forty five calendar columns.

00:00:32.000 --> 00:00:36.570
Our question is how a tree turns this
declared input into a prediction.

00:00:38.817 --> 00:00:42.267
First ignore the hour and fit a constant.

00:00:42.757 --> 00:00:44.967
The four counts sum to three
hundred and seventy seven.

00:00:46.457 --> 00:00:51.267
Dividing by four gives ninety four
point two five rentals per hour.

00:00:51.717 --> 00:00:56.507
This is the squared error minimizing
constant for the whole tiny sample.

00:00:56.957 --> 00:01:01.657
It is much too high for the two quiet
observations and much too low for the busy

00:01:01.657 --> 00:01:02.187
ones.

00:01:02.657 --> 00:01:05.367
The issue is not a poor learning rate.

00:01:05.597 --> 00:01:10.807
This constant family has no way to make
the requested hour change its answer.

00:01:12.817 --> 00:01:18.057
A one split tree can ask whether the hour
eight indicator is at most zero point

00:01:18.057 --> 00:01:18.707
five.

00:01:19.097 --> 00:01:20.987
A zero takes the left branch.

00:01:21.417 --> 00:01:26.327
Its two supporting counts are one and two,
so its mean is one point five.

00:01:26.777 --> 00:01:28.527
A one takes the right branch,

00:01:28.657 --> 00:01:31.787
whose counts are one hundred and seventy
nine and one hundred and ninety five.

00:01:33.077 --> 00:01:34.127
Their mean is one
hundred and eighty seven.

00:01:35.617 --> 00:01:38.907
Asking for eight now returns
that right leaf value.

00:01:39.157 --> 00:01:42.407
The tree stores the question and
the two values during fitting,

00:01:42.717 --> 00:01:44.567
then reuses them during prediction.

00:01:46.858 --> 00:01:52.178
The source selects the hour eight position
from the four real encoded rows and keeps

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:53.348
targets separate.

00:01:53.878 --> 00:01:57.968
It asks the library for a depth one
regression tree with a fixed seed.

00:01:58.518 --> 00:02:01.108
Fitting constructs the
test and mean leaves;

00:02:01.558 --> 00:02:03.828
prediction receives only zero or one.

00:02:04.418 --> 00:02:08.878
The library searches useful threshold
splits rather than taking gradient steps

00:02:08.878 --> 00:02:11.168
on an existing linear weight vector.

00:02:11.798 --> 00:02:15.838
We use its implementation so the learning
focuses on the model contract and

00:02:15.838 --> 00:02:16.328
arithmetic.

00:02:17.258 --> 00:02:19.888
The project also saves inspectable tests,

00:02:20.278 --> 00:02:22.928
support counts and values
for the full models.

00:02:25.150 --> 00:02:28.836
The constant has total squared
error thirty four thousand,

00:02:28.836 --> 00:02:32.720
five hundred and thirty eight point
seven five on these four rows.

00:02:33.290 --> 00:02:34.800
Inside the busy leaf,

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:38.400
each outcome is eight rentals
away from its fitted mean,

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:40.420
contributing sixty four each.

00:02:41.130 --> 00:02:45.840
The two quiet errors have magnitude one
half, contributing one quarter each.

00:02:46.350 --> 00:02:49.400
The new total is one hundred
and twenty eight point five.

00:02:49.450 --> 00:02:53.000
A mean leaf does not make
its observations identical;

00:02:53.410 --> 00:02:55.280
it minimizes their squared deviations.

00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.600
This training improvement says
nothing yet about another date.

00:03:01.733 --> 00:03:05.223
Change the requested
hour from eight to nine.

00:03:05.733 --> 00:03:09.503
In this probe,
the only supplied indicator becomes zero.

00:03:10.113 --> 00:03:14.613
The tree therefore follows the same branch
as the two oclock observations and returns

00:03:14.613 --> 00:03:15.983
one point five.

00:03:16.133 --> 00:03:20.103
That is not a claim about
real nine oclock demand.

00:03:20.633 --> 00:03:24.063
It is what this deliberately limited
fitted function can distinguish.

00:03:24.633 --> 00:03:29.193
The full benchmark uses all forty
five calendar inputs and many more

00:03:29.193 --> 00:03:29.903
observations.

00:03:30.673 --> 00:03:34.903
It still cannot distinguish requests
whose available inputs are identical.

00:03:35.533 --> 00:03:39.873
Keep this limitation in mind before
interpreting deeper trees as automatic

00:03:39.873 --> 00:03:41.803
understanding of the future.

